  "final_text": "## Summary\n\nI ran EXCLURAD with your requested parameters for \u03b7 electroproduction:\n- **W** = 1.54 GeV\n- **Q\u00b2** = 1.3207 GeV\u00b2\n- **cos \u03b8*** = 0.341379\n- **\u03c6*** = 36\u00b0\n- **Standard CLAS12 settings**: beam 6.53 GeV, inelasticity cut 0.166 GeV\u00b2, electron, detected proton\n\n### Results\n\n| Parameter | Value |\n|-----------|-------|\n| **\u03c3_Born** | 0.0008651649 |\n| **\u03b4 (delta)** | NaN |\n\n### Issue\n\nEXCLURAD successfully computed the Born cross section but encountered numerical issues in the radiative correction integration, producing NaN for the radiative correction factor. Investigation revealed:\n\n1. **The kinematics point is valid**: Born cross section computed successfully, confirming the point is physically possible\n2. **The issue is Q\u00b2-specific**: Testing with Q\u00b2 = 0.7 GeV\u00b2 using the same W, cos \u03b8*, and \u03c6* produces valid **\u03b4 = 0.9020214298**\n3. **Integration failure**: The 3D radiative correction integral fails during tail (virtual photon) contribution calculation, specifically in multipole component 2\n4. **Within lookup table**: The point falls within EtaMAID-2023 table coverage (W = 1.4856\u20136.0 GeV, Q\u00b2 = 0\u20135.0 GeV\u00b2)\n\nThis appears to be a numerical/computational issue with radiative correction calculation for this specific Q\u00b2 value, not a physical impossibility or code error.\n\nThe `outcome.json` file has been written to the working directory with full details.",
